\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

\paragraph{Why temporal context dominates.}
Information-theoretic reasoning suggests that hidden states (4096 dimensions) should dominate text features (384 dimensions) by a wide margin. In practice, the temporal dimension matters more than signal richness. Text probes benefit \textit{more} from temporal context than HS probes (+12.0pp accuracy, +23.2pp precision from $W{=}1$ to $25$ vs.\ +3.2pp, +12.9pp for HS), strengthening the claim that the bottleneck is temporal rather than informational. Safety commitment is a gradual process: the model progressively consolidates intent over multiple steps, and observing this progression provides the dominant information gain.

\paragraph{Position as confound vs.\ genuine temporal signal.}
On QwQ-32B, the position-only baseline (70.6\% accuracy) slightly exceeds the HS probe at layer 2 (67.8\%), raising the question of whether temporal windowing gains reflect genuine safety dynamics or merely positional correlations. We note that selectivity (15.3pp) still exceeds the Hewitt threshold, confirming non-trivial safety signal. However, position information is likely a partial contributor to temporal windowing gains, particularly when the optimal probe layer is near the embedding. Disentangling position from safety content would require probes trained on position-shuffled sequences, which we leave for future work.

\paragraph{Architecture, scale, and distillation confound.}
R1-8B is simultaneously the only Llama-architecture model, the only 8B-parameter model, and the only model produced through knowledge distillation rather than native reasoning training. Any or all of these factors could explain its distinctive HS probe advantage. Distillation may compress safety-relevant information into more linearly separable representations; alternatively, the Llama architecture may organize safety features differently from Qwen, or smaller models may require more structured internal representations to maintain safety reasoning. Disambiguation requires testing additional configurations: a non-distilled Llama reasoning model (to isolate distillation), a distilled Qwen-8B (to isolate architecture), or a Llama-32B variant (to isolate scale). Until such comparisons are available, the R1-8B result should be interpreted as specific to that model rather than generalized to a model class.

\paragraph{Method boundaries.}
BOCPD achieves high coverage (93\%) but not early detection; CUSUM underperforms simple thresholding; TrajGuard's geometric approach fails because safe and jailbreak trajectories occupy indistinguishable activation regions; cross-model transfer fails entirely. These negative results define clear boundaries: safety commitment is gradual rather than abrupt, model-specific rather than universal, and invisible to geometric distance measures.

\paragraph{Design implications.}
Temporal windowing is the most robust default for safety monitoring research. Modality choice requires per-model validation: HS probes show clear advantages on R1-8B (76.8\% vs.\ 54.4\% precision), but on QwQ-32B text probes achieve lower FPR (17.8\% vs.\ 20.8\%) without meaningful accuracy sacrifice. Given that all methods produce 39--62\% FPR on safe traces, current probes characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than constitute production-ready detectors. The 85.5\% oracle gap indicates that passive monitoring captures only a fraction of the available early detection potential, motivating future work on active intervention methods.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
We presented the first systematic evaluation of safety commitment detection in reasoning models, comparing hidden-state probes, text monitors, temporal windowing, sequence models, and geometric baselines across three models, two architectures, and two scales. The central finding is that temporal context dominates modality choice, and that the residual modality advantage is model-specific rather than universal. Future directions include causal intervention through activation patching, expansion to multilingual and multi-turn settings, and evaluation on additional architectures to test whether model-specificity of safety representations generalizes.

